BUILDING
========
The native host (Linux x64, from the repository root):

    python3 tools/build.py --jobs 6

builds the pinned dependency snapshots in third_party/deps, then the native
Cycles host, the CPU references, the WGSL translation and the WebGPU backend.
`python3 native/tools/build_cycles.py --jobs 6` rebuilds only the Cycles host
targets once the dependencies exist. The host library lands at
native/work/cycles-build/lib/libcodebrix_cycles_host.so. Build outputs stay in
ignored work directories. Python 3, CMake, Make, a C/C++20 toolchain and Clang
(for the AST translation) are external build tools; NASM is optional (JPEG SIMD;
the verified build uses the scalar fallback). The unchanged
CodeBrix.WebGpu.Native.MitLicenseForever package (the exact version pinned by
the build scripts) must be in the normal NuGet cache or at
CODEBRIX_WEBGPU_PACKAGE, including its original archive for integrity
verification. Source fetches and sibling implementation checkouts are never
build inputs. Android, Linux ARM64 and osx-arm64 recipes are described in
native/host/README.md, LINUX_ARM64.md and MACOS_ARM64.md.

Reusing a copied build tree (lane worktrees seeded with another checkout's
native/work/cycles-build, prototype/work/cycles-build or
native/work/android/<rid>/host/build, together with the generated overlay and
dependency prefix next to them): pass --rebase-build-tree to tools/build.py,
native/tools/build_cycles.py, prototype/tools/build_host.py or
native/tools/build_cycles_android.py (tools/rebase_build_tree.py does the work).
It reads the old checkout root from CMakeCache.txt, moves the object folders
CMake names after absolute source paths, rewrites the old root in every text
file of the tree and in the referenced generated inputs under work/ (keeping
their modification times), gives every referenced source, header, CMake file
and library the modification time of the same file in the old checkout when
the two are byte-identical (the old checkout must still exist; it is read
only), then configures and builds as usual and checks that no build file still
names the old root and that every compiled source is under this checkout.
Files that differ from the old checkout rebuild, with everything that depends
on them; an unchanged tree rebuilds nothing and produces byte-identical
binaries. A copy that was already stale in the old checkout (objects older than
their inputs there) rebuilds exactly what the old checkout would have rebuilt.
Relinked binaries never match the old checkout byte for byte: CMake's build
RUNPATH names the dependency prefix of the checkout that linked them. The
overlay generator writes generated includes only when their content changes.

The .NET solution:

    dotnet restore CodeBrix.Graphics3D.Cycles.slnx
    dotnet build   --configuration Release CodeBrix.Graphics3D.Cycles.slnx

Target framework net10.0 only. The build must stay at 0 warnings / 0 errors;
TreatWarningsAsErrors is on in the library project. GenerateDocumentationFile
is on, so CS1591 is fixed by writing the doc comment, never with NoWarn.
AllowUnsafeBlocks is on because the wrapper pins spans for the P/Invokes.


TESTING
=======
    tests/CodeBrix.Graphics3D.Cycles.Tests - xUnit v3,
    xunit.runner.visualstudio, Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk and SilverAssertions.
    No coverage collector is referenced.

The test runner IS Microsoft.Testing.Platform, selected by the root global.json.
Do not delete that file: without it `dotnet test` falls back to the VSTest
bridge, which fails on the .NET 10 SDK.

    dotnet test --solution CodeBrix.Graphics3D.Cycles.slnx

If `dotnet test` ever reports zero tests, run the built test assembly directly
and quote its counts:

    dotnet build tests/CodeBrix.Graphics3D.Cycles.Tests -c Release
    dotnet tests/CodeBrix.Graphics3D.Cycles.Tests/bin/Release/net10.0/CodeBrix.Graphics3D.Cycles.Tests.dll

What the tests need:
  - NativeHeaderConstantMatching parses native/host/cycles_host.h from the
    working tree and checks every managed enum value against its CBC_*
    constant. It needs only the source tree.
  - CyclesHostTests exercise the wrapper against the native host built in this
    working tree (native/work/cycles-build/lib/libcodebrix_cycles_host.so) on
    the CPU reference backend; NativeHostLibrary registers a
    DllImport resolver that loads that file. When it is absent, or the host is
    not Linux x64, those tests SKIP with the reason (they never fail for a
    missing native build). Build the native host first to run them.
  - CyclesSceneTests, CyclesAbiTests, CyclesImageTests, CyclesVersionTests,
    CapabilityReportTests and NodeTypeTests use the same native host and skip
    the same way (CapabilityReportTests' bare-device-report parse runs without
    it). They cover the ABI 1 additions on the CPU reference backend: native
    progress events, cancellation through the event callback's return value,
    node deletion and its reference check, image re-upload, pass readback,
    edits after a render (bit-identical to a scene built with them), the
    version structure and the capability report. The test
    project sets AllowUnsafeBlocks for the raw callback test in CyclesAbiTests; NodeTypeTests' generator check runs
    `python3 tools/managed/generate_model.py --check` and skips when python3
    is not available.
  - WebGpuRenderTests render the shared validation scenes
    (tools/hosttests/WebGpuScenes/WebGpuValidationScenes.cs: glass sphere,
    diffuse with a sun, textured plane through image upload) and the procedural
    glTF sample on CyclesBackend.Gpu and on CyclesBackend.CpuReference, require
    the practical criterion and print the maximum error and strict differences
    (test output; `-showLiveOutput` when running the test assembly directly),
    check progress events, cancellation and reuse on the GPU backend and the
    capability report's tier decision. They skip with a reason when no WebGPU
    adapter is available. They also recover a scene after a rejected render
    (a depth pass) and move an object after a render (Phase E3 semantics).
    Run them once with a cold scratch
    MESA_SHADER_CACHE_DIR after changing GPU code (the whole class takes about
    a minute cold on the Intel UHD laptop).
  - GltfSceneConverterTests drive the glTF example client
    (tools/gltf/CycleGltfRender, referenced by the test project; it brings the
    CodeBrix.Graphics3D.Gltf2.MitLicenseForever package into the tests only) on
    the CPU reference backend with a procedural sample model; no glTF asset is
    stored in the repository.
  - The tests never call ConfigureColor or ConfigureTiers, which are
    process-wide; the host gate covers those.

The native validation is separate from `dotnet test`. The maintained Linux host
gate rebuilds the native targets, generates 786 independent CPU reference films,
and runs the C and managed lifecycle, color-reload, concurrency and GPU
lifecycle checks, plus the ABI test (abi_consumer.c, CPU and WebGPU) and the
ABI.md example (example.c), and checks the 48 exports:

    python3 native/tools/host_gate.py --output native/work/<new-folder>

Use a new output folder for each run (allow about 20 minutes). The other gates
in native/tools/ are documented in the native documents listed above. The SVM
probe gates compile scene-specialized modules, like the renderer. The
specialization on/off film check uses the superset mode
(CODEBRIX_CYCLES_SHADER_SPECIALIZATION=superset:G, size-bounded groups):
glass_gate.py, principled_gate.py and nodes_gate.py (nodes_world and
nodes_render) run every group by default and require bit-identical films
(--off-mode unspecialized selects the whole unspecialized module, a diagnostic
that exceeds the Intel Mesa 25.0.7 16-bit instruction/register range;
--off-mode none skips the check). native/tools/unspecialized_compile_smoke.py
records the unspecialized module size and cold compile time as warnings only.
After a WGSL change, dump the modules a gate compiles
(CODEBRIX_CYCLES_WGSL_DUMP=DIR) and run native/tools/intel_compile_envelope.py
on them: every entry point must stay below 60,000 backend instructions and
registers (native/DEVICE.md, "Linux reference fixes on the updated Mesa").
The product carries the same limit as a warning: every pipeline build measures
the inlined WGSL size of each entry point it compiles (pipelines.h, size guard)
and warns at 1,200,000 bytes (about 60,000 backend instructions at the measured
central density of 20 bytes per instruction; 16.9 to 22.1 measured on Mesa
25.0.7). The warning goes to stderr before the compile, into the device
report's "warnings" (so ICapabilityReport.Warnings) with a "sizeGuard" object
(threshold, largest entry, estimate), and into the process report of the gates.
It never changes the variant, the tier or the "degraded" flag.
CODEBRIX_CYCLES_PIPELINE_SIZE_WARN_BYTES overrides the threshold (0 disables).
native/tools/pipeline_size_guard_check.py is its Linux gate check (a render gate
run at the default and at a lowered threshold; films must be bit-identical).
The specialization feature mask (pipelines.h ShaderFeatures) is a fixed array of
64-bit words (256 bits); bit numbers keep their meaning once assigned (0-61 and
63 are in use, 62 and 64 onwards are free); reports and logs carry feature
names, never the numeric mask, so widening it changes no persisted format.

Testing on other machines follows the per-platform procedures in docs/:
TESTING-MACOS-ARM64.md (Apple Silicon, Metal), TESTING-MACOS-X64.md (Intel
Mac, WebGPU) and TESTING-LINUX-ARM64.md (Raspberry Pi 5, WebGPU). Each lists
the build commands, the ordered gates with budgets, the reference policy, what
to record and the evidence to bring back.

Licensing and source checks:

    python3 tools/licensing/audit.py
    python3 tools/licensing/notices.py --check
    (cd tools/compatibility && python3 -m unittest test_inventory -q)
    (cd tools/licensing/tests && python3 -m unittest test_guards -q)

audit.py requires every active code file (outside evidence/ and third_party/),
including every .cs file, to carry an SPDX-License-Identifier line and the
original-contribution copyright line. That is why the .cs files in src/ and
tests/ start with the two-line header described under CODING CONVENTIONS.


GENERATED OBJECT MODEL
======================
src/CodeBrix.Graphics3D.Cycles/Generated/ is produced by
tools/managed/generate_model.py from Generated/registry.json, a formatted
snapshot of cbc_host_registry_json (CyclesHost.RegistryJson()) taken from the
native host built in this tree. The generator is deterministic, needs only
Python 3 and runs offline; its output is checked in.

    python3 tools/managed/generate_model.py            # regenerate from the snapshot
    python3 tools/managed/generate_model.py --check    # fail when out of date

After a native change that alters the registry, rebuild the host, capture the
registry and regenerate:

    python3 native/tools/build_cycles.py --jobs 6
    (capture CyclesHost.RegistryJson() to native/work/<folder>/registry.json with
     a small program that registers a DllImport resolver for the built host)
    python3 tools/managed/generate_model.py --registry native/work/<folder>/registry.json --update-registry

then build (0 warnings) and run the tests; NodeTypeTests compares the catalogue
with the snapshot and the live registry, checks --check, and round-trips every
generated property through the native host.

Generation rules: one class per node type, named <PascalCase native name>Node
(a small rename table in the script covers the light, point-cloud and
geometry-base names); folders and namespaces by category (Scene, Geometry,
Lights, Shaders); one property per readable, writable, non-internal input,
typed from the registry socket type; one enum per enum socket; ShaderInput /
ShaderOutput accessors (named after the socket UI names, which the native link
call uses) for shader graph nodes; NodeType.cs carries every type and socket
with its factory. The few hand-maintained members (shader graph Output, Add and
Connect, geometry attributes, mesh triangles and deformation motion, texture
Bind and Unbind) are the
EXTRAS table in the script and delegate to CyclesScene. Generated files carry
the two-line copyright/SPDX header and an <auto-generated> banner; every
public member has an XML doc comment generated from the registry (native
name, UI name, socket type), so CS1591 stays satisfied without suppression.


HOST-GATE FIXTURES
==================
    tools/hosttests/HostLifecycle/   managed lifecycle, property, registry,
                                     tier, cancellation and disposal checks
    tools/hosttests/ImageModes/      managed image-mode film checks
    tools/hosttests/ColorReload/     six A/B/C/A/B/C color configuration rounds
    tools/hosttests/AndroidHost/     the Android validation-app template
    tools/hosttests/WebGpuScenes/    WebGpuValidationScenes.cs: the WebGPU
                                     validation scenes, compiled into the test
                                     project and the Android app
    tools/hosttests/NuGet.Config     clears every package source for fixture
                                     builds

These are console/app fixtures driven by the gate scripts, not family tests and
not in the solution. native/tools/host_gate.py builds HostLifecycle, ImageModes
and ColorReload with tools/hosttests/NuGet.Config and runs them;
native/tools/macos_host_gate.py builds HostLifecycle. They keep their own
project settings (they are fixtures, not shipped code).

ANDROID VALIDATION APP
----------------------
AndroidHost is a template, not a standalone build:
native/tools/build_android_app.py copies it, generates callable checks from
HostLifecycle/Program.cs and ImageModes/Program.cs, and builds a diagnostic APK
that consumes the wrapper and native host on Android ARM64 and x64 (minimum API
33). By default the app runs the explicit CPU reference suite below. Launched
with the intent extra `mode=webgpu` (android_app_gate.py --mode webgpu) it
instead renders the three WebGPU validation scenes (glass sphere, diffuse with a
sun, textured plane through image upload; WebGpuValidationScenes.cs, shared with
the Linux tests) on CyclesBackend.Gpu and on CyclesBackend.CpuReference inside
the app, compares the films with the repository criteria, checks progress
events and cancellation-then-reuse on the GPU backend, and records the
capability report (pipeline tier decision, compile budget, warnings). The
builder copies WebGpuChecks.cs and the shared scenes file with the template.

From the repository root, first build both Android RIDs with
`python3 tools/build_android.py --rid android-arm64` and
`python3 tools/build_android.py --rid android-x64`. Generate independent Linux
references with `python3 native/tools/host_gate.py --output
native/work/host-validation`. A failed reference-validation run must be
investigated; the app builder requires a passed reference record and verifies
its pinned outputs. Existing validated reference directories may be reused
while their fixture inputs still match. Build and inspect a new APK with:

    python3 native/tools/build_android_app.py \
      --output native/work/android-app/validation-v1 \
      --references native/work/host-validation

The builder audits the current native inputs when --native-review is omitted.
An existing matching review directory can be supplied explicitly. The recipe
requires the installed Linux x64 build tools, the pinned NDK, the .NET
SDK/Android workload and runtime-pack versions recorded in the script, Java and
the Android build-tools recorded there. NuGet feeds are cleared for this test
build. No renderer or dependency implementation source is fetched.

Run on an explicitly selected attached device after other native tests on that
device have completed:

    python3 native/tools/android_app_gate.py \
      --build-output native/work/android-app/validation-v1 \
      --output native/work/android-app/device-v1 \
      --device tablet,android-arm64,DEVICE_SERIAL

Repeat --device LABEL,RID,SERIAL for additional devices. Use a new output
directory for each build/run. The runner refuses to replace an
already-installed validation app and force-stops its own installation after
collecting output. To leave an installed validation app untouched, build with
`build_android_app.py --application-id com.codebrix.cycles.hostvalidation.<suffix>`
(the runner reads the id from the build status) and pass `--uninstall` to the
runner to remove its own installation after the evidence is extracted.

The WebGPU mode, with a result timeout matching the declared device budget:

    python3 native/tools/android_app_gate.py --mode webgpu \
      --result-timeout 2400 --uninstall \
      --build-output native/work/android-app/validation-v1 \
      --output native/work/android-app/device-webgpu-v1 \
      --device tablet,android-arm64,DEVICE_SERIAL

It recomputes every GPU-versus-CPU comparison from the extracted film bytes,
requires practical agreement for all three scenes and for the re-render after
cancellation, records strict differences, and saves the capability report as
capability-report.json per device. The source-tree pins of the native build,
the app build and both gates must match: do not edit pinned sources between the
Android native build and the device runs.

Validation reference policy (native/DEVICE.md): add `--linux-reference DIR` to
judge each device GPU film against the Linux x64 (glibc) CPU film of the same
scene; the in-app CPU film (Android's bionic C library) is then recorded as a
diagnostic. Produce DIR on Linux with

    CODEBRIX_CYCLES_REFERENCE_EXPORT=$PWD/native/work/managed-reference \
      dotnet test --project tests/CodeBrix.Graphics3D.Cycles.Tests/CodeBrix.Graphics3D.Cycles.Tests.csproj \
      -c Release --filter-method "*gpu_film_matches_the_cpu_reference_with_the_practical_criterion*"

The native device gates follow the same policy through
native/tools/android_reference.py (Linux export, device-scene reference and
host-side comparison) and the CODEBRIX_CYCLES_REFERENCE_* and
CODEBRIX_CYCLES_SCENE_* variables documented in
native/cycles/reference_policy.h; native/tools/libm_probe.py measures a device
C library against correctly rounded results.

The app tests registry and property readback, cancellation/reuse, disposal
during rendering, three lifecycle films and six A/B/C/A/B/C configuration
rounds totaling 1,560 image films. Cross-platform films retain
`5e-5 + 2e-4*abs(reference)` practical acceptance and
`1e-7 + 1e-7*abs(reference)` strict diagnostics. Lifecycle films also require
strict agreement. Same-process CPU repeat assertions remain bitwise. The runner
independently compares the saved film bytes and verifies installed native
hashes and app-private loading. Comparison of a serialized maximum-error
diagnostic alone allows one double ULP for Mono JSON formatting; that allowance
does not change either film threshold. Reports retain strict image differences.
Only staged copies lose native debug information; the shipped WebGPU binaries
are unchanged. APK inspection verifies native payloads, reference and notice
assets, runtime-pack provenance, minimum API and ELF segment alignment. These
are local diagnostic APKs, not release packages or proof of 16-KiB-page
execution.


PACKAGING AND PUBLISHING
========================
The library csproj carries the family's canonical date-stamped version block
(1.<years since 2026>.<UTC day of year>.<UTC minute of day>) and
GeneratePackageOnBuild, so every build writes a fresh
CodeBrix.Graphics3D.Cycles.ApacheLicenseForever.<version>.nupkg under
src/CodeBrix.Graphics3D.Cycles/bin/<Configuration>/. The package contains the
assembly, its XML documentation, icon-codebrix-128.png, README.md,
AGENT-README.txt and THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt.

The package does NOT yet contain native runtime assets (runtimes/<rid>/native)
and has not been published. Adding the reviewed native payloads from
native/binaries/<rid>/, and the package audit in tools/licensing/audit.py
(--package with a reviewed --payload-manifest, which also requires LICENSE and
NOTICE in the payload), are release prerequisites. Jeremy publishes; nobody
else pushes packages.


CODING CONVENTIONS
==================
- Every .cs file: the two-line header
      // Copyright (c) 2026 Jeremy Ellis and contributors
      // SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
  (required by tools/licensing/audit.py), one blank line, the using block
  (System.* first, then others, alphabetical, no blank lines inside, no global
  usings), one blank line, the file-scoped namespace, one blank line, the type.
- No nullable reference annotations: NRT is off; never write `?` on a
  reference type (string, object, classes) and never use the `!`
  null-forgiveness operator. Value-type nullables (int?, enum?) are fine.
- No <Nullable>, <ImplicitUsings>, <NoWarn> or <LangVersion> in the library
  csproj.
- XML doc comments on every public type and member, including every enum
  value.
- Entry points and shared value types stay in the root namespace
  CodeBrix.Graphics3D.Cycles; sub-folders are sub-namespaces (Rendering,
  Diagnostics, Nodes, Generated.*, Interop). Interop.Native and
  Interop.CyclesAbi are public and mirror the C ABI one to one: add members for
  new exports, never change existing signatures.
- Managed enum values are the C ABI constants; NativeHeaderConstantMatching
  fails when they drift. Change native/host/cycles_host.h and the enum
  together.
- Tests: <ClassUnderTest>Tests.cs, method names Member_snake_case or pure
  snake_case, //Arrange //Act //Assert comments, expression-bodied
  single-statement tests, SilverAssertions fluent assertions, and
  TestContext.Current.CancellationToken on any cancellable call.


PROVENANCE AND VENDORED SOURCES
===============================
The managed code in src/ and tests/ is original. The native renderer
incorporates a filtered, pinned Cycles source snapshot and pinned native
dependency snapshots under third_party/, with independent Apache-2.0
replacements for excluded upstream files. Every origin, pinned revision,
license, modification and exclusion is recorded in the third_party/ manifests;
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt and NOTICE are generated from them by
tools/licensing/notices.py. Never hand-edit the generated files.


PIPELINE CACHE (AUDIT OF THE SHIPPED RUNTIME)
=============================================
Driver shader compilation dominates first-render time and gate wall time. A
persistent pipeline cache was audited on 2026-10-22 against the shipped
CodeBrix.WebGpu.Native.MitLicenseForever 1.0.290.1433 runtime, which this
repository consumes unchanged (wgpu-native 29.0.1.1, wgpuGetVersion
0x1d000101; codebrix ABI 2). Result: the runtime exposes NO usable pipeline
cache on any backend, so the host and the managed API offer none. Nothing in
this repository pretends to cache.

What the runtime has:
- wgpu.h declares WGPUNativeFeature_PipelineCache (0x0003002B; header note:
  Vulkan supported, D3D12 and Metal unimplemented) and WGPUHubReport counts
  pipelineCaches. On the Linux Intel adapter (Mesa 25.0.7) and the HP Intel
  adapter (Mesa 24.0.8) the adapter advertises the feature and a device can
  be created with it enabled.
- Nothing can USE it. No RID's library (linux-x64/arm64/riscv64,
  android-x64/arm64, osx-x64/arm64, win-x64/arm64) exports a pipeline-cache
  function: no wgpuDeviceCreatePipelineCache, wgpuPipelineCacheGetData or
  release/add-ref. The 251 exports match WGPU_CODEBRIX_PROC_TABLE_FUNCTION_COUNT
  (251) in codebrix_wgpu_procs.h, which has no cache entry. wgpu.h has no
  sType for chaining a cache into WGPUComputePipelineDescriptor (the native
  sTypes stop at WGPUSType_SamplerDescriptorExtras, 0x0003000B), and
  WGPUComputePipelineDescriptor (webgpu.h) has no cache member.
  wgpuGetProcAddress ends the process (unimplemented). The Vulkan HAL inside
  the library loads vkCreatePipelineCache/vkGetPipelineCacheData internally;
  that path is not reachable from C.

Driver caches (below the runtime; measured, not configured by this repository):
- Linux, Intel Mesa 25.0.7: Mesa's on-disk cache works and fully covers our
  pipelines. Default location $XDG_CACHE_HOME/mesa_shader_cache_db (else
  ~/.cache/mesa_shader_cache_db), overridable with MESA_SHADER_CACHE_DIR,
  MESA_SHADER_CACHE_MAX_SIZE (Mesa default 1 GB), MESA_SHADER_CACHE_DISABLE.
  The host's WGSL is deterministic, so a second run hits: see native/DEVICE.md
  "Pipeline cache" for cold/warm walls (films bit-identical). Consumers on
  Linux therefore get warm compiles from the second run on, without anything
  from us. The gate runners deliberately use a fresh scratch
  MESA_SHADER_CACHE_DIR per run, so every wall in the merge reports is COLD.
- HP (Android 13 x64, Bliss OS, Intel Mesa 24.0.8): NO driver cache. Neither
  MESA_SHADER_CACHE_DIR nor XDG_CACHE_HOME makes the driver write anything,
  and repeated compiles cost the same; the driver binary does not contain the
  MESA_SHADER_CACHE_DISABLE check (the runners' MESA_SHADER_CACHE_DISABLE=true
  is a no-op there). INFERENCE: Mesa's disk cache returns "disabled" on Android
  builds. Every run on the HP compiles cold.
- Samsung tablet (Adreno 730): no effective implicit cache observed (the S20
  and Pixel runs have no repeated pipeline to compare). In merge wave 4, principled_bounce compiles the same 5
  pipelines on 8 fresh devices in one process; each repeat costs as much as
  the first (Samsung 252 of its 290 compile seconds, HP 800 of 917, are
  repeats), and the same transparent_lighting pipeline cost 8.5 s and 8.2 s in
  two separate processes.
- macOS (Metal) and Windows (D3D12): not measured here. The runtime has no
  cache API on either (the header calls both unimplemented); the system/driver
  shader caches of those platforms are outside our control.

DECISION (maintainer, 2026-10-22): pipeline caching IS to be enabled. The
next time CodeBrix.WebGpu / CodeBrix.WebGpu.Native is updated for any reason,
that release must add the pipeline-cache exports listed below (Vulkan honours
them, which covers Linux and Android; the other backends return Unsupported),
and this repository then implements the host design described after the list
and removes the "no persistent cache" bullet from AGENT-README.txt. Nothing in
CodeBrix.WebGpu is changed before that update, and no workaround is built here
in the meantime. Put the runtime version that first ships the exports in
native/host/ABI.md when it exists, and raise the pin in this repository to it.

What a future runtime release would need to export (in CodeBrix.WebGpu.Native;
mirrors wgpu's Device::create_pipeline_cache,
PipelineCache::get_data, ComputePipelineDescriptor::cache and
wgpu::util::pipeline_cache_key):
- WGPUPipelineCache handle; WGPUPipelineCacheDescriptor { nextInChain,
  label, data pointer + size (initial blob, may be empty), fallback (WGPUBool:
  an invalid/foreign blob yields an empty cache instead of an error) };
  wgpuDeviceCreatePipelineCache(device, descriptor) (requires
  WGPUNativeFeature_PipelineCache);
- wgpuPipelineCacheGetData(cache, data, size) as a two-call size/fill query
  (or WGPUStatus + out size), wgpuPipelineCacheAddRef/Release;
- a chained WGPUComputePipelineDescriptorExtras { chain, cache } with a new
  native sType (and the render-pipeline equivalent);
- an adapter cache-key query (the pipeline_cache_key string: vendor, device,
  driver identity) so a host can name files per adapter/driver;
- proc-table entries and wgpuCodebrixGetProcStatus rows for all of them,
  statuses Implemented on Vulkan and Unsupported (never abort) elsewhere.
With that, the host design is: one cache file per (runtime version, adapter
key, module hash, specialization-record hash) under a configurable directory,
loaded with fallback=true (corrupt or foreign entries are ignored and
rewritten), saved after compilation, and reported in the capability report.


NOTES
=====
- The CPU reference backend is the oracle for the WebGPU backend; GPU feature
  coverage is still partial (see native/DEVICE.md and docs/PARITY.md).
- Color configuration and pipeline-tier configuration are process-wide in the
  native layer; keep tests that touch them out of the parallel xUnit suite.
- Keep the public signatures of CyclesHost and the root value types stable:
  the host-gate fixtures and the Android validation app compile against them.
- Every facade behaviour maps onto an existing native export; nothing is
  simulated in managed code. The ABI 1 additions (native/host/ABI.md) are
  wired as follows: cbc_host_version -> CyclesVersion; CBC_HOST_GPU,
  cbc_host_resolve_backend and cbc_host_backend -> Backend.Gpu,
  CyclesScene.ResolveBackend and CyclesScene.Backend; cbc_host_capabilities_json
  -> CapabilityReport (device fields from its host.deviceReport object);
  cbc_host_delete_node -> CyclesScene.Remove (CBC_INVALID becomes
  InvalidOperationException; the wrapper and, for a shader, its graph nodes
  are marked removed); cbc_host_image_update -> CyclesImage.Update;
  cbc_host_motion_positions -> MeshNode.SetMotionPositions (CBC_NOT_READY, a
  refused edit of a baked volume, becomes InvalidOperationException);
  cbc_host_set_event_callback -> CyclesScene.RenderEventReceived and
  RenderAsync progress/cancellation; cbc_host_pass_floats and
  cbc_host_render_passes_json -> AddPass, GetRenderedPasses, ReadPass.
- Render event callback design: every render (sync and async) takes a
  per-scene render lock, registers one [UnmanagedCallersOnly] static callback
  with a GCHandle to a per-render listener, renders, clears the callback and
  frees the handle. The native host never invokes a callback after
  cbc_host_render returns or after a clear/destroy returns, so freeing the
  handle there is safe. The callback returns nonzero when the render's token
  is cancelled or a handler threw (the exception is rethrown after the render).
  RenderAsync still re-sends cbc_host_cancel every ProgressInterval while the
  token is cancelled, for the window before the first native event.
- Device errors (ABI.md "Device errors"): the next render recovers a WebGPU
  device that is intact and re-uploads the scene; CyclesScene sets IsFaulted on
  a CBC_FAILED render and clears it on a successful one. Edits after a render
  (ABI.md "Edits after a render", native/host/host_edits.inc) restore baked
  geometry's authored attributes; native/tools/scene_edit_gate.py covers them.


================================================================================
END OF MAINTAINER-README
